  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1023 PM CDT TUE OCT 13 2026\n\nThe warm weather continues to be the main story through the\nremainder of the week as daytime highs are expected to run\nseveral degrees higher than what is typical for this time of\nyear. We will likely approach and potentially break high\ntemperature records Wednesday and Thursday as this persistent\nhigh pressure remains across the region. This pattern really\ndoesn't start to change for Central AL until Friday morning when\na trough slides through the Ohio Valley and into the Appalachians\nby Friday night. This trough will push a dry cold front\nsoutheastward through the region during the day on Friday,\nleading to a sharp drop in temperatures for Saturday and Sunday.\nWe're not really expecting much in the way of rainfall with this\nfrontal passage, so the main impact will be gusty winds and the\ndrop in temperatures. The high pressure that builds in behind the\nfront Saturday into Sunday will shift eastward over the Carolinas\nby Sunday morning leading to a wedge front setting up for the\nearly part of next week. This will support drier conditions\npersisting through at least Monday into Tuesday.\n\n25/Owen",
